1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Hugo: Hi Chantal. What did you think of the talk, then?

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:07,970
Chantal: Hi Hugo. I thought it was good once I'd moved seats.

3
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:10,900
Hugo: Oh - were the people beside you chatting or something?

4
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:16,786
Chantal: It wasn't that. I went early so that I'd get a seat and not have to stand,

5
00:00:16,786 --> 00:00:21,200
but then this guy sat right in front of me and he was so tall!

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,930
Hugo: It's hard to see through people's heads, isn't it?

7
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:27,188
Chantal: Impossible! Anyway, to answer your question,

8
00:00:27,188 --> 00:00:30,860
I thought it was really interesting

9
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:34,170
especially what the speaker said about the job market.

10
00:00:34,170 --> 00:00:38,376
Hugo: Me too. I mean we know we're going into a really competitive field

11
00:00:38,376 --> 00:00:41,460
so it's obvious that we may struggle to get work.

12
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:46,560
Chantal: That's right - and we know we can't all have that 'dream job'.

13
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:50,020
Hugo: Yeah, but it looks like there's a whole range of

14
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:53,486
areas of work that we hadn't even thought of -

15
00:00:53,486 --> 00:00:55,060
like fashion journalism, for instance.

16
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:58,850
Chantal: Yeah - I wasn't expecting so many career options.

17
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:02,980
Hugo: Mmm. Overall, she had quite a strong message, didn't she?

18
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:07,578
Chantal: She did. She kept saying things like 'I know you all think this,

19
00:01:07,578 --> 00:01:11,190
but...' and then she'd tell us how it really is.

20
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:14,900
Hugo: Perhaps she thinks students are a bit narrow-minded about the industry.

21
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:19,080
Chantal: It was a bit harsh, though! We know it's a tough industry.

22
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:23,490
Hugo: Yeah - and we're only first years, after all. We've got a lot to learn.

23
00:01:23,490 --> 00:01:30,260
Chantal: Exactly. Do you think our secondary-school education should have been more career-focused?

24
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:33,340
Hugo: Well, we had numerous talks on careers

25
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:35,542
which was good, but none of them were very inspiring.

26
00:01:35,542 --> 00:01:39,650
They could have asked more people like today's speaker to talk to us.

27
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:43,919
Chantal: I agree. We were told about lots of different careers -

28
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:49,740
just when we needed to be, but not by the experts who really know stuff.

29
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:54,900
Hugo: So did today's talk influence your thoughts on what career you'd like to take up in the future?

30
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:57,880
Chantal: Well, I promised myself that I'd go through this course

31
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:00,250
and keep an open mind till the end.

32
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:03,796
Hugo: But I think it's better to pick an area of the industry now

33
00:02:03,796 --> 00:02:06,160
and then aim to get better and better at it.

34
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:10,260
Chantal: Well, I think we'll just have to differ on that issue!

35
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:12,077
Hugo: One thing's for certain, though.

36
00:02:12,077 --> 00:02:16,490
From what she said, we'll be unpaid assistants in the industry for quite a long time.

37
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:17,570
Chantal: Mmm.

38
00:02:17,570 --> 00:02:19,840
Hugo: I'm prepared for that, aren't you?

39
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:23,270
Chantal: Actually, I'm not going to accept that view.

40
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:28,530
Hugo: Really? But she knows it's the case - and everyone else says the same.

41
00:02:28,530 --> 00:02:31,170
Chantal: That doesn't mean it has to be true for me.

42
00:02:31,170 --> 00:02:35,240
Hugo: OK. Well - I hope you're right!

43
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:39,540
Chantal: I thought the speaker's account of her first job was fascinating.

44
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:44,394
Hugo: Yeah - she admitted she was lucky to get work being a personal dresser for a musician.

45
00:02:44,394 --> 00:02:46,880
She didn't even apply for the job

46
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,820
and there she was getting paid to choose all his clothes.

47
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:52,274
Chantal: It must have felt amazing -

48
00:02:52,274 --> 00:02:54,780
though she said all she was looking for back

49
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:58,410
then was experience, not financial reward.

50
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:00,820
Hugo: Mmm. And then he was so mean

51
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:04,820
telling her she was more interested in her own appearance than his!

52
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:10,242
Chantal: But - she did realise he was right about that, which really made me think.

53
00:03:10,242 --> 00:03:13,520
I'm always considering my own clothes

54
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:17,110
but now I can see you should be focusing on your client!

55
00:03:17,110 --> 00:03:19,640
Hugo: She obviously regretted losing the job.

56
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:25,990
Chantal: Well, as she said, she should have hidden her negative feelings about him, but she didn't.

57
00:03:25,990 --> 00:03:30,878
Hugo: It was really brave the way she picked herself up and took that job in retail.

58
00:03:30,878 --> 00:03:32,890
Fancy working in a shop after that!

59
00:03:32,890 --> 00:03:37,189
Chantal: Yeah - well, she recommended we all do it at some point.

60
00:03:37,189 --> 00:03:42,205
I guess as a designer you'd get to find out some useful information,

61
00:03:42,205 --> 00:03:45,430
like how big or small the average shopper is.

62
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:48,569
Hugo: I think that's an issue for manufacturers, not designers.

63
00:03:48,569 --> 00:03:52,849
However, it would be useful to know if there's a gap in the market -

64
00:03:52,849 --> 00:03:57,130
you know, an item that no one's stocking but that consumers are looking for.

65
00:03:57,130 --> 00:03:59,560
Chantal: Yeah, people don't give up searching

66
00:04:00,260 --> 00:04:03,920
They also take things back to the store if they aren't right.

67
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:06,803
Hugo: Yeah. Imagine you worked in an expensive shop

68
00:04:06,803 --> 00:04:10,780
and you found out the garments sold there were being returned because they

69
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:12,570
fell apart in the wash!

70
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:15,390
Chantal: Yeah, it would be good to know that kind of thing.
